<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Document</title>
</head>
<body>
    <script>
   
   function getUser(id){
    return new Promise((resolve,reject)=>{
        setTimeout(()=>{
           resolve({id:id,name:'John'}) 
        },500)
    })
   }
   function getPosts(userId){
    return new Promise((resolve,reject)=>{
        setTimeout(()=>{
            resolve([{id:1,userId:userId}])
        },500)
    })
   }
   function getComments(postId){
    return new Promise((resolve,reject)=>{
        setTimeout(()=>{
            resolve([{id:1,postId:postId}])
        },500)
    })
   }
getUser(1).then((user)=>{
     return getPosts(user.id)
}).then((posts)=>{
     getComments(posts[0].id)   // <-- no return!
}).then((comments)=>{
     console.log("Final comments: i am the person with id 1", comments)
})
    </script>    

</body>
</html>
<!--1.3 — Callback hell (build it on purpose)
Write three functions: getUser(id, cb), getPosts(userId, cb), getComments(postId, cb) — each fakes a 500ms delay then returns dummy data.
 Chain them the ugly way: nested callbacks 3 levels deep.
 This is intentional pain — you need to feel it before Promises make sense.-->
